    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebf9963d_bda97f80",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/system.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 878,
      "author": {
        "id": 1121210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-08T05:43:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please see the instructions which are the end of system_download_from_flash function.\n\n100abdb8:\tf840 7022 \tstr.w\tr7, [r0, r2, lsl #2]\n100abdbc:\t3201      \tadds\tr2, #1\n100abdbe:\te7f6      \tb.n\t100abdae \u003csystem_download_from_flash+0x76\u003e\n100abdc0:\t4628      \tmov\tr0, r5\n100abdc2:\t4633      \tmov\tr3, r6\n100abdc4:\te8bd 47f0 \tldmia.w\tsp!, {r4, r5, r6, r7, r8, r9, sl, lr}\n100abdc8:\t4718      \tbx\tr3\n100abdca:\tbf00      \tnop\n\nThe compiler uses \"bx r3\" jump to suspend ram. Setting bit 0 of address to 1 is necessary if CPU executes \"bx\" instruction. Or we\u0027ll get hard fault exception.",
      "parentUuid": "360a375e_b86d38c3",
      "revId": "d5915f40ad2ac8252c0b27def083f4f01d479ac2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f68dffb6_ec95b3eb",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/system.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 878,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-08T13:50:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You are relying on the compiler to emit those instructions. It is thumb mode you are trying to achieve. As such you need to put in the inline assembly to force the bx instruction as you are relying on it. That or comment very heavily the assumptions you are making such that when things blow up there\u0027s some pieces to look at after the fact.",
      "parentUuid": "ebf9963d_bda97f80",
      "revId": "d5915f40ad2ac8252c0b27def083f4f01d479ac2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebf9963d_bdff3ff9",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/system.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 878,
      "author": {
        "id": 1121210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-09T03:46:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The cortex-m4 only supports ARMv7E-M thumb instruction set. Since \"bx\" means branch and exchange instruction set, it uses the bit 0 of address to distinguish which mode it wants to exchange.\n \nBack to the question itself, generally, the compiler will use \"bx\" instruction for \"function pointer\" and vector ISRs because it doesn\u0027t know whether callee jumps back to caller. \"bx\" won\u0027t store pc to lr and compiler also handles the bit 0 of address automatically.\n\nBut in this case, we cast a 32-bits integer into a function pointer directly. The compiler won\u0027t handle address bit 0 for us. We need to add it by ourselves. I don\u0027t think we need to inline assembly. The reason I listed the instructions is just want to explain why setting bit 0 of address to 1.",
      "parentUuid": "f68dffb6_ec95b3eb",
      "revId": "d5915f40ad2ac8252c0b27def083f4f01d479ac2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b1e22a7_cb194931",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/system.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 878,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-09T17:41:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That reasoning doesn\u0027t make sense at all to me. A function pointer does not mean \"it may never return\". All functions return in C unless you indicate to the compiler otherwise.\n\nThat ldmia is reloading the link register. This is a tail call optimization in that it expects the callee to return back to this function which would subsequently return back to the caller.\n\nThe reality is that you are wanting to force a thumb branch. Since the core expects it to be thumb mode. Just comment that you are requiring thumb mode (thus the lowest bit set). I still think it\u0027d be better to provide clearer constructs through inline assembly or inline functions which have \u0027| 0x1\u0027 in one place, but a comment should suffice.",
      "parentUuid": "ebf9963d_bdff3ff9",
      "revId": "d5915f40ad2ac8252c0b27def083f4f01d479ac2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
